1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,300
How do you turn random noise into data?

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:05,038
Flow matching answers
with a velocity field.

3
00:00:05,088 --> 00:00:07,750
A neural network learns,
at every point and

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,150
at every time, which way
to move and how fast.

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,675
To generate, you start from
noise and follow the arrows.

6
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:15,913
But there is a puzzle.

7
00:00:15,963 --> 00:00:19,762
The network is trained on a target
that it can never match exactly.

8
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:23,212
Let us see what it really
learns, and why that works.

9
00:00:23,466 --> 00:00:26,178
We use the simplest
case, in one dimension.

10
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:29,704
Time runs from zero to one
along the horizontal axis.

11
00:00:29,754 --> 00:00:34,778
At time zero we have noise, x zero,
drawn from a standard Gaussian.

12
00:00:34,828 --> 00:00:37,116
At time one we have data, x one.

13
00:00:37,166 --> 00:00:39,229
Here the data are just two points,

14
00:00:39,279 --> 00:00:43,253
minus two and plus two, each
with probability one half.

15
00:00:43,303 --> 00:00:45,916
Pick one noise sample and one data point,

16
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:48,678
independently, and join
them with a straight line.

17
00:00:48,728 --> 00:00:55,203
At time t, the point on the line is one
minus t times x zero, plus t times x one.

18
00:00:55,253 --> 00:00:59,578
Its velocity is the time
derivative, x one minus x zero.

19
00:00:59,628 --> 00:01:04,391
It is the same at every time, so the
point moves at a constant speed.

20
00:01:04,642 --> 00:01:08,217
Training uses exactly this
velocity as the target.

21
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:11,467
Sample a time, a noise
point, and a data point.

22
00:01:11,517 --> 00:01:13,780
Form the point x t on their line.

23
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:17,367
The network sees x t
and t, and must predict

24
00:01:17,417 --> 00:01:20,679
x one minus x zero, under a squared error.

25
00:01:20,729 --> 00:01:23,580
This is the conditional
flow matching loss.

26
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:25,405
Now draw many such pairs.

27
00:01:25,455 --> 00:01:27,392
The straight lines cross each other.

28
00:01:27,442 --> 00:01:30,830
Look at the point x equals
zero, at time one half.

29
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:35,530
One line passes through it with slope plus
four, and another with slope minus four.

30
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:38,017
The same input gets two different targets.

31
00:01:38,067 --> 00:01:40,480
So the loss can never reach zero.

32
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:42,142
So what does the network learn?

33
00:01:42,192 --> 00:01:44,393
For a squared error, the best prediction

34
00:01:44,443 --> 00:01:47,093
for an input is the
average of its targets.

35
00:01:47,143 --> 00:01:51,318
Here, the average is over all lines
that pass through x at time t.

36
00:01:51,368 --> 00:01:54,630
We call it the marginal
velocity, u t of x.

37
00:01:54,680 --> 00:02:00,880
It is the conditional expectation of x one
minus x zero, given that x t equals x.

38
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:02,855
Now expand the loss around it.

39
00:02:02,905 --> 00:02:06,905
The first term is the distance from
the network to the marginal velocity.

40
00:02:06,955 --> 00:02:09,618
The second term is the
variance of the targets,

41
00:02:09,668 --> 00:02:11,530
and it does not depend on the network.

42
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:15,530
The cross term is zero, because
the targets average to u t.

43
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:19,367
So our loss has the same
gradients as the marginal loss,

44
00:02:19,417 --> 00:02:21,593
which in general we cannot compute.

45
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:23,595
Why is u t the field we want?

46
00:02:23,645 --> 00:02:26,808
Look at the distribution
of x t at a few times.

47
00:02:26,858 --> 00:02:32,270
It starts as one Gaussian, splits into two
bumps, and ends on the two data points.

48
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,820
All lines that end at the
same data point carry

49
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:38,858
a conditional distribution,
p t of x given x one.

50
00:02:38,908 --> 00:02:42,258
The marginal velocity averages
these line velocities,

51
00:02:42,308 --> 00:02:45,546
weighted by how likely each
line is at that point.

52
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:49,895
The continuity equation is linear
in density times velocity.

53
00:02:49,945 --> 00:02:52,033
So this weighted average moves the whole

54
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:54,808
noise distribution into
the data distribution.

55
00:02:54,858 --> 00:02:57,608
Following u t from noise gives data.

56
00:02:57,874 --> 00:03:01,074
Let us check this with real
numbers, at time one half.

57
00:03:01,124 --> 00:03:06,174
At x equals zero, the two lines have
targets plus four and minus four.

58
00:03:06,224 --> 00:03:11,049
By symmetry they are equally likely,
so the marginal velocity is zero.

59
00:03:11,099 --> 00:03:15,299
The network learns zero here, a
value it never saw as a target.

60
00:03:15,349 --> 00:03:17,624
Now move to x equals one half.

61
00:03:17,674 --> 00:03:20,574
The line toward plus two
has target plus three.

62
00:03:20,624 --> 00:03:23,749
The line toward minus two
has target minus five.

63
00:03:23,799 --> 00:03:26,024
But the posterior weights are not equal.

64
00:03:26,074 --> 00:03:28,974
They are about ninety eight
percent and two percent.

65
00:03:29,024 --> 00:03:34,249
So the marginal velocity is about two
point eight six, pointing toward plus two.

66
00:03:34,514 --> 00:03:38,151
Do this at every point, and we
get the whole marginal field.

67
00:03:38,201 --> 00:03:44,139
In closed form, it is the expected data
point, minus x, divided by one minus t.

68
00:03:44,189 --> 00:03:46,889
Now follow the field
from many noise samples.

69
00:03:46,939 --> 00:03:50,551
These marginal paths are
curved, and they do not cross.

70
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:54,489
Near the middle they hesitate, then
split toward the two data points.

71
00:03:54,539 --> 00:03:56,152
Compare the training lines.

72
00:03:56,202 --> 00:03:58,414
Those are straight, but they cross.

73
00:03:58,464 --> 00:04:02,751
The marginal flow is a deterministic
map from noise to data.

74
00:04:03,018 --> 00:04:05,918
To generate, draw x
zero from the Gaussian.

75
00:04:05,968 --> 00:04:09,580
Then solve the differential
equation, d x over d t

76
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:14,180
equals v theta of x and t,
from time zero to time one.

77
00:04:14,230 --> 00:04:16,805
The simplest solver is Euler's method.

78
00:04:16,855 --> 00:04:22,130
Take a step of size h along the current
velocity, then evaluate the network again.

79
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:27,493
Start at x zero equals zero point four,
with four steps of size one quarter.

80
00:04:27,543 --> 00:04:31,668
The positions are zero point
three, zero point three seven,

81
00:04:31,718 --> 00:04:34,293
one point zero nine, and finally two.

82
00:04:34,343 --> 00:04:37,193
The sample lands on the
data point plus two.

83
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,138
What if we take only one step?

84
00:04:39,188 --> 00:04:43,063
At time zero, x t carries no
information about the data.

85
00:04:43,113 --> 00:04:46,838
So the marginal velocity
is the data mean minus x.

86
00:04:46,888 --> 00:04:51,038
Here the mean is zero, so the
velocity is minus x zero.

87
00:04:51,088 --> 00:04:55,375
One Euler step sends every sample
to zero, the mean of the data.

88
00:04:55,425 --> 00:04:57,075
That is not a data point at all.

89
00:04:57,125 --> 00:05:00,513
Straight training lines do not
give straight marginal paths.

90
00:05:00,563 --> 00:05:04,050
This curvature is why the
sampler needs several steps.

91
00:05:04,298 --> 00:05:05,161
To recap.

92
00:05:05,211 --> 00:05:09,685
The network learns a velocity
field, v theta of x and t.

93
00:05:09,735 --> 00:05:14,048
It regresses on the per-sample
target, x one minus x zero,

94
00:05:14,098 --> 00:05:16,460
along straight lines from noise to data.

95
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:18,573
The squared error makes its optimum the

96
00:05:18,623 --> 00:05:21,348
conditional average,
the marginal velocity.

97
00:05:21,398 --> 00:05:25,073
That field moves the noise
distribution to the data distribution.

98
00:05:25,123 --> 00:05:28,486
The loss stays above zero by
the variance of the targets.

99
00:05:28,536 --> 00:05:31,198
To sample, integrate the
differential equation

100
00:05:31,248 --> 00:05:34,436
from noise at time zero
to data at time one.

